+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Pandas sits at the center of the Python data science ecosystem. Raw data flows in from files, and Pandas DataFrames feed into NumPy arrays for math, scikit-learn for ML models, Matplotlib for visualization, and PyTorch for deep learning. It is the universal data exchange format.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body">In NLP, your raw data might be a CSV with columns like <code>text</code>, <code>label</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, and <code>author</code>. NumPy cannot handle this directly -- it would force everything to strings. Pandas keeps each column in its natural type, lets you filter by label, group by author, and extract the text column as a list for tokenization. This is why <strong>every NLP pipeline starts with Pandas</strong>.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: Series
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Series</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>A Series is a one-dimensional labeled array.</strong> Think of it as a single column of a spreadsheet -- it has values AND an index (row labels). Under the hood, the values are stored as a NumPy array, so all NumPy operations work on Series too.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Creating Series</strong> -- from lists, dicts, and scalars:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># From a list -- auto integer index (0, 1, 2, ...)</span>\ns1 = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>([<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">40</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s1)\n<span class="cm"># 0    10</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1    20</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2    30</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3    40</span>\n<span class="cm"># dtype: int64</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># With custom index -- named labels!</span>\nscores = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>(\n    [<span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">87</span>, <span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">78</span>],\n    index=[<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"RL"</span>],\n    name=<span class="str">"exam_scores"</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores[<span class="str">"NLP"</span>])     <span class="cm"># 95 -- label-based access</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores.iloc[<span class="num">0</span>])    <span class="cm"># 95 -- position-based access</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># From a dictionary -- keys become index</span>\nages = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>({<span class="str">"Alice"</span>: <span class="num">25</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>: <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>: <span class="num">28</span>})\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ages[<span class="str">"Bob"</span>])       <span class="cm"># 30</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># From a scalar (broadcast to all indices)</span>\ndefaults = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>(<span class="num">0.0</span>, index=[<span class="str">"a"</span>, <span class="str">"b"</span>, <span class="str">"c"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(defaults)\n<span class="cm"># a    0.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># b    0.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># c    0.0</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Vectorized operations</strong> -- just like NumPy, no loops needed:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code>scores = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>([<span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">87</span>, <span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">78</span>], index=[<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"RL"</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># Arithmetic -- applies to every element</span>\ncurved = scores + <span class="num">5</span>              <span class="cm"># add 5 to every score</span>\nnormalized = scores / <span class="num">100</span>        <span class="cm"># scale to [0, 1]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Aggregations</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores.<span class="fn">mean</span>())    <span class="cm"># 88.0</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores.<span class="fn">std</span>())     <span class="cm"># 7.39</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores.<span class="fn">max</span>())     <span class="cm"># 95</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores.<span class="fn">idxmax</span>())  <span class="cm"># "NLP" -- label of the max value!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Boolean filtering</span>\npassing = scores[scores >= <span class="num">90</span>]   <span class="cm"># NLP: 95, DL: 92</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># String operations (for string Series)</span>\nnames = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>([<span class="str">"alice"</span>, <span class="str">"bob"</span>, <span class="str">"carol"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(names.str.<span class="fn">upper</span>())     <span class="cm"># ALICE, BOB, CAROL</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(names.str.<span class="fn">len</span>())       <span class="cm"># 5, 3, 5</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(names.str.<span class="fn">contains</span>(<span class="str">"o"</span>))  <span class="cm"># False, True, True</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Handling missing data (NaN)</strong> in Series:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># NaN = Not a Number -- Pandas\' missing value marker</span>\ns = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>([<span class="num">1.0</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">3.0</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">5.0</span>])\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">isna</span>())        <span class="cm"># [False, True, False, True, False]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())  <span class="cm"># 2 -- count missing values</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">dropna</span>())      <span class="cm"># [1.0, 3.0, 5.0] -- remove NaNs</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">fillna</span>(<span class="num">0</span>))     <span class="cm"># [1.0, 0.0, 3.0, 0.0, 5.0] -- replace NaN</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">fillna</span>(s.<span class="fn">mean</span>()))  <span class="cm"># fill with mean (3.0)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># NaN propagates through arithmetic</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">sum</span>())    <span class="cm"># 9.0 -- NaN is skipped by default!</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(s.<span class="fn">mean</span>())   <span class="cm"># 3.0 -- computed over non-NaN values</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">.values</div><div class="card-body">Returns the underlying NumPy array. Use when you need to pass data to NumPy or scikit-learn functions.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">.index</div><div class="card-body">Returns the index (row labels). Can be integers, strings, dates, or any hashable type.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">.dtype</div><div class="card-body">The data type of the Series: int64, float64, object (strings), bool, datetime64, category.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">.name</div><div class="card-body">Optional label for the Series. Becomes the column name when you insert a Series into a DataFrame.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">.shape</div><div class="card-body">Tuple with one element: (n,). Consistent with NumPy -- useful for shape assertions.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">.size</div><div class="card-body">Total number of elements (including NaN). Equal to len(series).</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Series vs NumPy 1D array:</strong> They look similar, but Series has <strong>alignment by index</strong>. When you add two Series, Pandas aligns them by label first, then computes. If labels do not match, the result is NaN -- this prevents silent data corruption that would happen with positional arrays.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: DataFrame Creation
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. DataFrame Creation</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>A DataFrame is a two-dimensional labeled table.</strong> Think of it as a dictionary of Series that share the same index. Each column is a Series, and the DataFrame manages them together -- like an in-memory SQL table or Excel spreadsheet.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Method 1: From a dictionary of lists</strong> (most common):</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Keys become column names, values become column data</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>:  [<span class="str">"Mikail"</span>, <span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"David"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>:   [<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">22</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">95.5</span>, <span class="num">88.0</span>, <span class="num">92.3</span>, <span class="num">79.8</span>, <span class="num">91.0</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"RL"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>]\n})\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df)\n<span class="cm">#     name  age  score field</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  Mikail   25   95.5   NLP</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1   Alice   30   88.0    CV</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2     Bob   28   92.3    RL</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3   Carol   35   79.8   NLP</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4   David   22   91.0    DL</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Method 2: From a list of dictionaries</strong> (common with JSON/API data):</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Each dict is a row -- great for JSON-style data</span>\nrecords = [\n    {<span class="str">"text"</span>: <span class="str">"Great movie!"</span>, <span class="str">"label"</span>: <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="str">"conf"</span>: <span class="num">0.95</span>},\n    {<span class="str">"text"</span>: <span class="str">"Terrible film"</span>, <span class="str">"label"</span>: <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="str">"conf"</span>: <span class="num">0.12</span>},\n    {<span class="str">"text"</span>: <span class="str">"OK movie"</span>, <span class="str">"label"</span>: <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="str">"conf"</span>: <span class="num">0.45</span>},\n]\ndf2 = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(records)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df2)\n<span class="cm">#             text  label  conf</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0   Great movie!      1  0.95</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1  Terrible film      0  0.12</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2       OK movie      0  0.45</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Method 3: From a NumPy array</strong> (when you need column names for raw data):</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># NumPy array + column names = DataFrame</span>\ndata = np.random.<span class="fn">default_rng</span>(<span class="num">42</span>).<span class="fn">standard_normal</span>((<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">3</span>))\ndf3 = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(data, columns=[<span class="str">"feature_1"</span>, <span class="str">"feature_2"</span>, <span class="str">"feature_3"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df3.<span class="fn">round</span>(<span class="num">2</span>))\n<span class="cm">#    feature_1  feature_2  feature_3</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0       0.30      -1.34      -1.57</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1      -0.72       0.36      -0.81</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2       0.66       1.85      -0.98</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3       0.27      -0.15       0.68</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4      -0.32      -0.39      -0.07</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Method 4: From CSV</strong> (the real-world default):</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># The most common way to get a DataFrame in practice</span>\ndf4 = pd.<span class="fn">read_csv</span>(<span class="str">"sentiment_data.csv"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Useful parameters</span>\ndf4 = pd.<span class="fn">read_csv</span>(\n    <span class="str">"data.csv"</span>,\n    encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>,        <span class="cm"># for Turkish characters</span>\n    usecols=[<span class="str">"text"</span>, <span class="str">"label"</span>],  <span class="cm"># only load these columns</span>\n    nrows=<span class="num">10000</span>,             <span class="cm"># only first 10k rows</span>\n    dtype={<span class="str">"label"</span>: <span class="str">"int8"</span>}  <span class="cm"># force dtype to save memory</span>\n)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Columns, Index, and dtypes:</strong> Every DataFrame has three key properties: <code>df.columns</code> (column labels), <code>df.index</code> (row labels), and <code>df.dtypes</code> (type per column). Columns are always accessible by name. The index defaults to 0, 1, 2, ... but can be set to any meaningful label (dates, IDs, names).</div>'

