+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> A DataFrame has three components: the <strong>index</strong> (row labels, highlighted in teal), the <strong>columns</strong> (column headers, highlighted in gold), and the <strong>values</strong> (the actual data). Each column has its own dtype: name is object (string), age is int64, score is float64, field is object.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: Basic Inspection
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Basic Inspection</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The first 3 commands on any new dataset:</strong> <code>df.shape</code>, <code>df.info()</code>, <code>df.describe()</code>. These tell you the size, types, missing values, and distribution in seconds. Master these before touching the data.</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Sample DataFrame for inspection</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>:  [<span class="str">"Mikail"</span>, <span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"David"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>:   [<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">22</span>, <span class="num">27</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">95.5</span>, <span class="num">88.0</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">79.8</span>, <span class="num">91.0</span>, <span class="num">85.5</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"RL"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># head() and tail() -- peek at the data</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">head</span>(<span class="num">3</span>))   <span class="cm"># first 3 rows (default: 5)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">tail</span>(<span class="num">2</span>))   <span class="cm"># last 2 rows</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># shape -- (rows, columns)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.shape)     <span class="cm"># (6, 4)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># columns -- column names</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.columns)   <span class="cm"># Index([\'name\', \'age\', \'score\', \'field\'])</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># index -- row labels</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.index)     <span class="cm"># RangeIndex(start=0, stop=6, step=1)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># dtypes -- type of each column</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.dtypes)\n<span class="cm"># name      object</span>\n<span class="cm"># age        int64</span>\n<span class="cm"># score    float64</span>\n<span class="cm"># field     object</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># info() -- the MOST important inspection method</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">info</span>()\n<span class="cm"># &lt;class \'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame\'&gt;</span>\n<span class="cm"># RangeIndex: 6 entries, 0 to 5</span>\n<span class="cm"># Data columns (total 4 columns):</span>\n<span class="cm">#  #   Column  Non-Null Count  Dtype</span>\n<span class="cm"># ---  ------  --------------  -----</span>\n<span class="cm">#  0   name    6 non-null      object</span>\n<span class="cm">#  1   age     6 non-null      int64</span>\n<span class="cm">#  2   score   5 non-null      float64   &lt;-- 1 missing!</span>\n<span class="cm">#  3   field   6 non-null      object</span>\n<span class="cm"># memory usage: 320.0+ bytes</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># describe() -- statistics for numeric columns</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">describe</span>())\n<span class="cm">#             age      score</span>\n<span class="cm"># count  6.000000   5.000000   &lt;-- score has 5, not 6!</span>\n<span class="cm"># mean  27.833333  87.960000</span>\n<span class="cm"># std    4.355109   5.917770</span>\n<span class="cm"># min   22.000000  79.800000</span>\n<span class="cm"># 25%   25.250000  85.500000</span>\n<span class="cm"># 50%   27.500000  88.000000</span>\n<span class="cm"># 75%   29.500000  91.000000</span>\n<span class="cm"># max   35.000000  95.500000</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps"><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">value_counts()</div><div class="step-detail">Count occurrences of each unique value in a column. Essential for understanding categorical distributions.<br><code>df["field"].value_counts()</code> shows NLP: 2, CV: 2, RL: 1, DL: 1</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">nunique()</div><div class="step-detail">Number of unique values per column. Useful for identifying categorical vs continuous features.<br><code>df.nunique()</code> -- name: 6, age: 6, score: 5, field: 4</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">memory_usage(deep=True)</div><div class="step-detail">Actual memory consumption per column in bytes. Use <code>deep=True</code> for accurate string memory. Critical for large datasets that push RAM limits.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">isna().sum()</div><div class="step-detail">Count missing values per column. Always check this before training a model -- most ML algorithms cannot handle NaN directly.</div></div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># value_counts -- frequency of each unique value</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[<span class="str">"field"</span>].<span class="fn">value_counts</span>())\n<span class="cm"># NLP    2</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV     2</span>\n<span class="cm"># RL     1</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL     1</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># nunique -- how many unique values per column</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">nunique</span>())\n<span class="cm"># name     6</span>\n<span class="cm"># age      6</span>\n<span class="cm"># score    5</span>\n<span class="cm"># field    4</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Missing value summary</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())\n<span class="cm"># name     0</span>\n<span class="cm"># age      0</span>\n<span class="cm"># score    1</span>\n<span class="cm"># field    0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># memory_usage with deep=True for accurate string sizes</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">memory_usage</span>(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>))\n<span class="cm"># Index     128</span>\n<span class="cm"># name     358</span>\n<span class="cm"># age       48</span>\n<span class="cm"># score     48</span>\n<span class="cm"># field    342</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Inspection Workflow EN --- */
+ '<div id="plot-inspect-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
